    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7c91085a-ea33-5bc7-b784-9a3e6858a972",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{1},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries below $a_{11}=1$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}-R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{3}:=R_{3}-2R_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{2}:=R_{2}-R_{1}$ and $R_{3}:=R_{3}-2R_{1}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
